  Line 12:  {category:'How genetic information is used',title:'Replication and inheritance',text:'Before many cells divide, DNA polymerases copy DNA using each parental strand as a template. Replication is generally semiconservative: each daughter molecule contains one old strand and one newly synthesized strand. Origins, enzymes, proofreading, and chromosome-end maintenance differ among organisms. DNA is transmitted through cell division and reproduction, but inheritance is shaped by recombination, chromosome segregation, developmental biology, and environment—not by sequence alone.'},

  Line 14:  {category:'How genetic information is used',title:'Translation and the genetic code',text:'Ribosomes read messenger RNA in three-base codons and, with transfer RNAs, assemble amino acids into a polypeptide. The standard genetic code maps most codons to amino acids and includes start and stop signals. It is nearly universal, but not absolutely: some organisms and organelles use variant codes. Proteins then fold, may be modified, and interact with other molecules; the DNA sequence alone does not specify every detail of a cell’s final state.'},

  Line 15:  {category:'Variation and regulation',title:'Noncoding DNA and gene regulation',text:'Noncoding DNA includes regulatory sequences, introns, structural regions, repeated elements, and sequences with unknown or context-dependent functions. Promoters and enhancers can influence when, where, and how strongly genes are transcribed, often through transcription factors and chromatin state. “Noncoding” does not mean “nonfunctional,” and sequence conservation or biochemical activity alone does not prove a specific biological function.'},

